    "description": "Pars Khodro is an Iranian vehicle manufacturer with documented links to SAIPA and Renault-based production. Its history illustrates why corporate ownership, manufacturing location and the brand attached to a vehicle need to be distinguished. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Pars Khodro", "SAIPA", "Renault", "Tondar", "Sandero", "Vehicle manufacturing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:28:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:28:04Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Pars Khodro: SAIPA Links, Renault Production and Data | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Pars Khodro's ownership history, Renault manufacturing role and annual production, with explicit limits on unresolved evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "ownership-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A distinct manufacturer within a wider network",
        "content": "Pars Khodro should be understood as a manufacturing company with its own history and reporting identity, even when its vehicles or ownership connect it to a larger group. A Financial Times report from March 2000 recorded a major share disposal in which SAIPA and Iran's Social Security Investment Company jointly acquired a controlling block offered by the state holding company. The report described a transaction presented as privatization while questioning how far effective state influence had changed. [s1]\n\nThat distinction is useful beyond this particular sale. A company can have shares traded or transferred without every source of public-sector influence disappearing. The identity of shareholders, their own ownership and the right to appoint management all affect the practical result. The 2000 report supports this historical caution; it cannot establish Pars Khodro's present ownership. A later Tehran Stock Exchange account explicitly described Pars Khodro as a SAIPA subsidiary. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "renault-manufacturing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Renault connection",
        "content": "Pars Khodro's Renault connection helps explain the difference between a vehicle brand and its manufacturer. The exchange's account identified the Tondar 90 as a Renault Logan assembled through the relationship involving SAIPA, Pars Khodro and Renault Pars. A Renault badge therefore did not mean that a finished vehicle had necessarily been imported from France. [s2]\n\nA February 2019 report from Croatia's foreign ministry identified Iran Khodro and SAIPA–Pars Khodro as Iranian manufacturers of Renault vehicles, associating the latter with Logan and Sandero production. It documented a substantial contraction in Renault manufacturing during the preceding year and placed that deterioration in the context of United States sanctions. The same report also noted independent analysts' criticism of management at major Iranian manufacturers. External restrictions and domestic organizational performance should consequently be examined separately rather than assuming that either explanation accounts for every outcome. [s3]\n\nThese historical relationships do not demonstrate that the same licensed arrangements remained active at the editorial cutoff. Nor does the continued presence of an older vehicle on Iranian roads establish that its original foreign partner continued supplying or supervising its production. A current account requires later contractual and operational evidence. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Separate ownership from the manufacturing arrangement",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  S[\"SAIPA\"] -->|\"Described as parent in 2010\"| P[\"Pars Khodro\"]\n  P -->|\"Participated in assembly\"| T[\"Tondar 90\"]\n  R[\"Renault Logan\"] -->|\"Iranian market identity\"| T\n  J[\"Renault Pars relationship\"] -->|\"Joint-venture framework\"| T\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates the historically reported parent relationship from a vehicle's design lineage and assembly framework. It is not a current ownership chart. [s2]",
        "as_of_date": "2010-07-24",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-production",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the production series with its source caveat",
        "content": "The annual figures below preserve JETRO's historical table. Its separate model table gives a conflicting total for 1402, so that observation remains unresolved. The series should support cautious description rather than a precise growth claim until the underlying filing is checked. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
